From c4b591e5c8be1f831acdc1366ee5870feaa3f699 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vuln Patch Pipeline <vuln-patch@chainguard.dev>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 20:33:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Reject absolute request paths in Webserver.doReply

Webserver.doReply() stripped the "GET /" prefix from the request line
and passed the rest to checkFilename(). checkFilename() rejected ".."
and some characters, but not a leading slash. A request such as
"GET //etc/passwd" therefore named an absolute path. When htmlfileBase
was null, the server read that file and returned it, so a client could
read any file the JVM could read (CWE-36).

doReply() now throws BadHttpRequest when the request path is absolute,
before htmlfileBase is applied. It also resolves the target file and
the document root (htmlfileBase, or the working directory when it is
null) to canonical paths. It throws BadHttpRequest unless the file is
inside the root, so a symlink in the root that points outside it is
also refused. Relative requests inside the root, the classpath
fallback for .class files and the doReply() signature are unchanged.

Adds WebserverTraversalTest.

Cgp-Finding: CGP-8339-r3p5-6gh4
